      { title: "Class lifecycle and JVM execution", explanation: "A class is loaded, verified, prepared, resolved as needed, and initialized before first active use. Bytecode begins in an interpreter or lower compilation tier while runtime profiles identify hot methods and branches. The JIT may inline, eliminate allocations, and specialize code using assumptions; invalid assumptions cause deoptimization back to safer code, so warm-up behavior can differ sharply from steady state.", keyPoints: ["Loading and initialization are distinct; static initialization is synchronized per class.", "JIT optimization uses observed profiles and may later be revoked.", "Microbenchmarks need a harness such as JMH to control warm-up and dead-code elimination.", "Bytecode portability does not imply identical performance across JVMs or collectors."] },
      { title: "Memory management and garbage collection", explanation: "Objects become collectible when they are unreachable from GC roots, not when a scope visually ends. Most JVMs allocate quickly in thread-local buffers and collect young objects frequently because most die young. Collector choice trades throughput, pause behavior, CPU, memory overhead, and operational predictability; a memory leak in managed code is usually unintended reachability rather than missing free calls.", keyPoints: ["Stack versus heap is an implementation-oriented explanation; escape analysis can change allocation.", "finalize is unsafe and obsolete; use ownership protocols such as try-with-resources.", "A larger heap can reduce collection frequency while increasing worst-case recovery or scan costs.", "Heap pressure, allocation rate, retained size, and pause time are different measurements."] },
      { title: "Concurrency and the Java Memory Model", explanation: "The Java Memory Model defines when writes by one thread become visible to another and which reorderings are legal. Happens-before edges arise from program order, monitor release/acquire, volatile write/read, thread start/join, and library synchronization. volatile provides visibility and ordering for a variable but does not make compound read-modify-write operations atomic; correctness begins with an invariant and a synchronization policy that covers every participating field.", keyPoints: ["A data race is not merely two threads executing at once; it is conflicting unsynchronized access.", "volatile is appropriate for independent state publication, flags, and some carefully designed algorithms.", "Locking protects invariants, not individual lines of code.", "ConcurrentHashMap makes its own operations safe but cannot atomically protect arbitrary multi-step workflows."] }
