      { prompt: "Does volatile make an object thread-safe? Explain using a stop flag and a counter.", difficulty: "hard", answer: "volatile applies to reads and writes of the field and creates ordering/visibility edges. It works for a one-writer stop flag whose state is independent. A volatile counter increment is still multiple operations and loses updates; use an atomic, lock, confinement, or redesigned invariant.", whyTricky: "Visibility, atomicity, and compound object invariants are collapsed into one vague idea of thread safety.", rubric: ["Defines the volatile guarantee.", "Contrasts a valid flag with invalid increment.", "Names a correct alternative and invariant scope."] },
      { prompt: "What happens from java Main to optimized machine code?", difficulty: "hard", answer: "The launcher creates a JVM, loads and verifies classes, links and initializes them, then executes bytecode initially through interpretation or lower tiers. Runtime profiles identify hot paths; tiered JIT compilers inline and specialize into machine code. Guard failure or class loading can invalidate assumptions and trigger deoptimization.", whyTricky: "Bytecode execution is often simplified to either pure interpretation or one-time compilation, omitting profiling and reversible optimization.", rubric: ["Covers class lifecycle and bytecode verification.", "Explains profiling and tiered JIT.", "Mentions speculative optimization and deoptimization."] },
      { prompt: "A Java service's memory grows although GC runs. How do you distinguish healthy growth, pressure, and a leak?", difficulty: "hard", answer: "Observe post-GC live-set trend, allocation rate, pause/CPU behavior, and workload. Healthy warm-up plateaus; pressure has high churn or insufficient headroom; a leak shows retained live data growing across comparable full collections. Compare heap histograms and dominator/retained paths to GC roots, then verify the owning lifecycle.", whyTricky: "Heap size, allocation, and retention are different signals, while dashboards often show only total used memory.", rubric: ["Uses post-GC live set rather than one snapshot.", "Separates allocation pressure from retention.", "Explains retained-path investigation and lifecycle ownership."] },
      { prompt: "When can parallel streams be slower or incorrect?", difficulty: "medium", answer: "They can be slower for small inputs, cheap operations, poor spliterators, ordered pipelines, boxing, blocking work, or a contended common pool. They become incorrect when operations interfere with the source, mutate shared state, or use non-associative reductions whose grouping changes the result.", whyTricky: "The API makes parallelism syntactically trivial while its cost model and algebraic requirements remain hidden.", rubric: ["Names workload and pool overheads.", "Explains stateless/non-interfering requirements.", "Explains associativity for reduction."] },
      { prompt: "Why might @Transactional appear to do nothing in Spring?", difficulty: "hard", answer: "Spring commonly applies transactions through a proxy. Self-invocation bypasses that proxy; private/final methods or unsupported proxy arrangements may not be intercepted; checked-exception rollback differs by policy; and crossing threads loses thread-bound transaction context. Verify proxy entry, propagation, exception policy, and database boundaries.", whyTricky: "The annotation is mistaken for a language feature rather than metadata interpreted at a runtime interception boundary.", rubric: ["Identifies proxy/self-invocation behavior.", "Covers rollback or propagation semantics.", "Connects transaction context to thread and database work."] }
